' Runtime test: a USER-DECLARED resource lives in a collection, exactly as a
' built-in one does.
'
' `mfb spec` §15.6 gives a collection element the `RES` marker precisely so a
' resource can be stored in one, and `List OF RES fs::File` has always worked.
' The identical program over a declared `RESOURCE Db` did not BUILD:
'
'   error: native collection packed payload does not support type 'Db'
'
' No code, no file, no line. Cause: `is_pointer_collection_payload_type` asked
' `builtins::is_resource_type`, which answers for the BUILT-IN registry only, so
' a declared resource was not recognised as an 8-byte pointer payload and fell
' through the packed-payload dispatch to its error arm. That is bug-546's family
' — a builtin-only classifier — at a third site, after the flatness walk
' (bug-546) and the thread-handle arm (bug-479).
'
' WHAT THIS ASSERTS. `count=4` is the layout half: four handles were appended and
' the list agrees. `used=4` is the sharper half — every element is read back out
' of the list and used, so a wrong stride or alignment would hand `sql::exec` a
' misaligned or aliased pointer and the SUCCESS_ON gate would raise. Each
' iteration creates a DIFFERENTLY-NAMED table, so two elements aliasing the same
' database would also fail rather than pass quietly.
IMPORT io
IMPORT collections

RESOURCE Db CLOSE BY sql::close

LINK "sqlite3" AS sql
  FUNC open(path AS String) AS RES Db
    SYMBOL "sqlite3_open"
    ABI (path CString, db OUT CPtr) AS status CInt32
    RETURN db
    SUCCESS_ON status = 0
  END FUNC
  FUNC exec(RES db AS Db, statement AS String) AS Nothing
    SYMBOL "sqlite3_exec"
    ABI (db CPtr, statement CString, callback CPtr, arg CPtr, errmsg CPtr) AS status CInt32
    CONST callback = NOTHING
    CONST arg = NOTHING
    CONST errmsg = NOTHING
    SUCCESS_ON status = 0
  END FUNC
  FUNC close(RES db AS Db) AS Nothing
    SYMBOL "sqlite3_close"
    ABI (db CPtr) AS status CInt32
    SUCCESS_ON status = 0
  END FUNC
END LINK

FUNC main AS Integer
  MUT dbs AS List OF RES Db = []
  MUT i AS Integer = 0
  WHILE i < 4
    RES d AS Db = sql::open(":memory:")
    dbs = collections::append(dbs, d)
    i = i + 1
  END WHILE
  io::print("count=" & toString(len(dbs)))
  MUT j AS Integer = 0
  WHILE j < len(dbs)
    sql::exec(collections::get(dbs, j), "CREATE TABLE t" & toString(j) & "(a INTEGER);")
    j = j + 1
  END WHILE
  io::print("used=" & toString(j))
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
